ef 17592186045417] [17592186423030 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}-x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
